<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Irtysh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Irtysh River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Irtysh is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, about 4,250 kilometres long, which rises in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai Mountains</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, flows through Kazakhstan and joins the Ob in western Siberia. Its valley was home to steppe peoples such as the Kimek and the Naimans, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qashliq">Qashliq</a>, capital of the Khanate of Sibir, stood on its banks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omsk">Omsk</a>, Pavlodar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semey">Semey</a> are among the cities on its course.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/irtysh.jpg" alt="Irtysh" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Irtysh_river_basin_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
